WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:04.212
Something is happening in Texas.

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:06.006
Since 2021, there have been

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:08.341
31 separate lawsuits, in federal district

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:10.093
courthouses all over the state,

00:00:10.093 --> 00:00:13.346
in which Texas has sued the federal government.

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:17.058
Let's look at just one, in Amarillo, about immigration.

00:00:17.058 --> 00:00:19.608
The judge in this case - there was no jury - 

00:00:19.608 --> 00:00:22.147
ruled that the Biden administration had to continue

00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:25.066
a Trump policy called "Remain in Mexico"

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:28.820
that keeps asylum seekers out of the country.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.363
The Biden administration eventually

00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:32.949
got that decision reversed, but it took almost a whole year.

00:00:32.949 --> 00:00:35.869
And as they waited for the Supreme Court to do that,

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:38.204
they also had to obey the decision

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:41.624
and keep asylum seekers out of the country.

00:00:41.624 --> 00:00:44.502
All because of this one judge's ruling.

00:00:44.502 --> 00:00:45.128
And in fact,

00:00:45.128 --> 00:00:47.922
the Texas Attorney General's office, which filed the case,

00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:51.134
had sought this judge out specifically.

00:00:51.134 --> 00:00:53.678
And over the next two years, they would bring him cases

00:00:53.678 --> 00:00:58.308
against the federal government again, and again, and again.

00:00:58.308 --> 00:00:59.684
And he's not alone.

00:00:59.684 --> 00:01:05.231
Texas has gone back to this judge again and again too.

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:06.566
You're not supposed to be able

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:08.443
to pick which judge hears your case.

00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:11.362
But in Texas, you can.

00:01:11.362 --> 00:01:13.406
It's called "judge shopping."

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:15.283
It's only possible in a place like Texas.

00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:19.537
And it's helped make Texas into a powerful weapon

00:01:19.537 --> 00:01:21.790
for changing how things work

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:25.126
everywhere in the country.

00:01:26.836 --> 00:01:30.131
Each state in the US has at least one
federal district court.

00:01:30.131 --> 00:01:31.341
Texas has four.

00:01:31.341 --> 00:01:34.344
They're the bottom level of the federal court system.

00:01:34.344 --> 00:01:35.678
Federal courts mostly hear cases

00:01:35.678 --> 00:01:38.223
involving national laws. And federal judges

00:01:38.223 --> 00:01:39.474
are appointed by the president.

00:01:39.474 --> 00:01:43.436
There are over 600 district
judges across all of these courts.

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:44.979
So several judges in each court.

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:48.942
And usually the judge who hears your case is chosen
randomly.

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:51.694
STEVE VLADECK: Randomness is a critical principle

00:01:51.694 --> 00:01:54.072
because the idea is that the judicial system

00:01:54.072 --> 00:01:56.533
is supposed to be, on the whole,

00:01:56.533 --> 00:01:59.202
a neutral arbiter of legal disputes.

00:01:59.202 --> 00:02:01.454
But the US is big.

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:02.956
There are a lot of big states.

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:05.917
And even when a state has multiple federal court districts,

00:02:05.917 --> 00:02:08.461
some of those districts are still really big.

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:11.756
Like this one, the Northern District of Texas.

00:02:11.756 --> 00:02:13.049
If you're in Lubbock,

00:02:13.049 --> 00:02:16.136
and your case gets randomly assigned to a judge in Dallas,

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:17.846
that's a five-hour drive to get to court.

00:02:17.846 --> 00:02:19.764
So partly to solve for that,

00:02:19.764 --> 00:02:23.434
many districts are further subdivided
into multiple divisions,

00:02:23.434 --> 00:02:25.395
and each usually has its own courthouse.

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:28.606
But different districts have different rules

00:02:28.606 --> 00:02:31.693
for which case goes to which division.

00:02:31.693 --> 00:02:34.988
For example, over in the Central District of California,

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:37.782
there are three divisions, with clear rules

00:02:37.782 --> 00:02:40.952
meant to make sure that cases stay local.

00:02:40.952 --> 00:02:42.620
But in the Northern District of Texas,

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.581
those rules aren't so strict.

00:02:44.581 --> 00:02:46.416
It's much easier to file a case in

00:02:46.416 --> 00:02:48.334
whatever division you choose.

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.712
Same with the other Texas districts.

00:02:50.712 --> 00:02:54.132
Here's where that becomes a problem.

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:55.425
The Southern District of Texas

00:02:55.425 --> 00:02:58.845
has 28 judges for seven divisions.

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:01.639
But for most of the last two years, two of those divisions

00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:05.268
have had just one active judge each.

00:03:05.268 --> 00:03:07.812
Here in the Victoria
Division, that's been judge Drew Tipton.

00:03:07.812 --> 00:03:11.441
And in the Galveston Division, it's Judge Jeffrey Brown.

00:03:11.441 --> 00:03:13.318
And this happens all over the state.

00:03:13.318 --> 00:03:14.736
In the Northern District, this judge,

00:03:14.736 --> 00:03:17.739
Matthew Kacsmaryk, is the only judge in the Amarillo
division.

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:18.823
And Judge Reed O'Connor

00:03:18.823 --> 00:03:21.743
is the only one for the Wichita Falls Division.

00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:23.828
And that means that if you choose to file your lawsuit

00:03:23.828 --> 00:03:28.082
in one of those places, you know who the judge will be.

00:03:28.082 --> 00:03:29.375
So you get a situation where,

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:31.544
over two years, the Texas attorney general files

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:32.795
eight separate lawsuits

00:03:32.795 --> 00:03:35.590
against the Biden administration in Victoria,

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:38.968
because they knew Judge Tipton would hear them.

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:42.388
Cases about the border wall, the minimum wage, gun laws...

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:45.516
VLADECK: Judge Tipton had sided with the state of Texas,

00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:47.936
had embraced a pretty broad view of why Texas

00:03:47.936 --> 00:03:50.563
could sue in the first place, and had issued nationwide

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:53.274
injunctions barring the Biden administration

00:03:53.274 --> 00:03:54.817
from carrying out its policies.

00:03:54.817 --> 00:03:57.779
So, yeah, I mean, I don't think there was much of a mystery

00:03:57.779 --> 00:04:00.114
about why they would choose Judge Tipton.

00:04:00.114 --> 00:04:01.658
The second thing that makes Texas unique

00:04:01.658 --> 00:04:04.452
is where it sits in this chart.

00:04:04.452 --> 00:04:08.164
If the losing side of a district judge's decision appeals,

00:04:08.164 --> 00:04:10.625
it goes up to one of the 12 Appeals Courts,

00:04:10.625 --> 00:04:12.543
also called the circuit courts.

00:04:12.543 --> 00:04:15.546
A decision appealed from there goes up to the Supreme Court.

00:04:15.546 --> 00:04:18.091
And all Texas district courts report up

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:20.426
to the Fifth Circuit Court, which,

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:23.054
with a big majority of judges appointed by Republican presidents,

00:04:23.054 --> 00:04:24.889
is the most notoriously conservative

00:04:24.889 --> 00:04:26.975
of all the circuit courts, and broadly speaking,

00:04:26.975 --> 00:04:30.520
very likely to agree
with even the most conservative rulings.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:34.148
We saw this when a private group filed a lawsuit in Amarillo

00:04:34.148 --> 00:04:35.775
seeking to ban abortion pills

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:38.319
that had been legal for 20 years.

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:41.781
In April 2023, Judge Kacsmaryk sided with the private group,

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:44.075
saying the US Food and Drug Administration had to roll back

00:04:44.075 --> 00:04:47.203
its approval of mifepristone for the whole country.

00:04:47.203 --> 00:04:49.330
The Fifth Circuit Court largely agreed, and the case

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:50.581
made it to the Supreme Court --

00:04:50.581 --> 00:04:53.376
which then, actually paused that decision.

00:04:53.376 --> 00:04:56.504
VLADECK: But, you know, at multiple points
we were hours away from this

00:04:56.504 --> 00:05:00.133
really shockingly broad and remarkable ruling,

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:02.802
that would have dramatically restricted access
to mifepristone

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:05.972
on a nationwide basis, going into effect.

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:08.349
And so, you know, I think the notion that like,

00:05:08.349 --> 00:05:10.476
hey, we walked up to the cliff but didn't fall

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:13.479
in, doesn't mean everything's okay.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:14.230
But while the Supreme Court

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:17.150
moved quickly to pause the mifepristone decision, often

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:18.609
it actually just sits on cases

00:05:18.609 --> 00:05:20.278
for months before it hears them,

00:05:20.278 --> 00:05:22.655
effectively allowing the lower court judge's decision

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:25.783
to dictate federal law during that time.

00:05:25.783 --> 00:05:27.702
Like in 2022, when Judge Tipton ruled

00:05:27.702 --> 00:05:29.245
that Biden couldn't reprioritize

00:05:29.245 --> 00:05:32.206
which undocumented immigrants to deport first.

00:05:32.206 --> 00:05:33.458
Almost a year later,

00:05:33.458 --> 00:05:36.044
we're still waiting for the Supreme Court to rule on that.

00:05:36.044 --> 00:05:39.005
And Tipton's ruling is still in effect.

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:41.090
VLADECK: Any good lawyer is going to try

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:44.218
to maximize the chances that their client's going to win.

00:05:44.218 --> 00:05:46.804
What's different about what's been happening lately

00:05:46.804 --> 00:05:49.265
is actually trying to stack the deck

00:05:49.265 --> 00:05:51.934
so that you're guaranteed of a specific outcome.

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:52.935
Judge shopping the way we see it

00:05:52.935 --> 00:05:55.605
today is a relatively new phenomenon.

00:05:55.605 --> 00:05:57.648
So - how do we fix it?

00:05:57.648 --> 00:05:59.025
Well, the district courts themselves

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:00.401
could decide to do things differently.

00:06:00.401 --> 00:06:02.779
In February, the Southern District of Texas changed

00:06:02.779 --> 00:06:04.364
Victoria's single-judge status.

00:06:04.364 --> 00:06:06.824
It now has two active judges.

00:06:06.824 --> 00:06:08.034
And since these are federal courts,

00:06:08.034 --> 00:06:09.452
Congress could also pass a law

00:06:09.452 --> 00:06:12.038
regulating the way cases are distributed.

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:13.164
There's a bill in Congress right now

00:06:13.164 --> 00:06:14.415
that would redirect any lawsuit

00:06:14.415 --> 00:06:16.125
that wants to block a federal policy

00:06:16.125 --> 00:06:18.628
to the courts in Washington, DC.

00:06:18.628 --> 00:06:19.545
Or the Supreme Court

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:22.173
could write new rules for the lower courts.

00:06:22.173 --> 00:06:26.052
But until then, anyone who wants a good shot

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:28.388
at getting a case before the Supreme Court

00:06:28.388 --> 00:06:32.016
knows that in Texas district courts,

00:06:32.016 --> 00:06:34.977
you can pick your judge.

